if (!port->queue_id || team_port_enabled(port))
    return;
Reason:
The condition incorrectly returns early when the port is enabled, causing the function to skip deletion and re‑addition of the port in the queue override list. This leads to list corruption when a disabled port (already removed from the list) changes priority and attempts to call `__team_queue_override_port_del` again.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!port->queue_id || !team_port_enabled(port))
    return;
Reason:
The corrected condition returns early only when the port is disabled or has no queue ID, ensuring that the port is properly removed and re‑added to the queue override list when its priority changes while the port is enabled. This prevents the duplicate deletion that caused list corruption.

